we've had the conversation before about  how there are sort of two justice  systems in our country and there's lots  of models of this right there's the idea  that there's a justice system for the  rich and a justice system for the poor  there's the idea that there's a justice  system for white people and another for  not white people prosecuted convicted  sentenced more harshly for many of the  same crimes particularly a lot of the  crimes that sort of fall under the  umbrella of the war on drugs there's a  justice system for corporations and  another for the little guy right the  Supreme Court for example finding much  more often in favor of corporate  interests than in favor of the  individual and that gets us to this week  you might remember this case from March  where Krystal Mason a black woman in  Texas was sentenced to five years in  prison for accidentally voting illegally  in the 2016 election she was on  probation on probation for a fraud  conviction meaning she was not allowed  to vote she voted and she was found  guilty and sentenced to five years in  prison there's another story from last  year about Rosa Maria Ortega who was  convicted of voting illegally in the  2012 general election and the 2014  Republican runoff in Texas she was  sentenced to eight years in prison and  in comparison remember two justice  systems Tarrant County Justice of the  Peace Russ Casey has pleaded guilty to  effectively rigging his own election he  signed off on signatures that he knew  were fake in order to get on the March  six primary ballot he received five  years probation not prison not Jail  so there's actually multiple angles here  right there's the sort of obvious race  angle which is crystal mason is black  Rosa Maria Ortega's Hispanic and Justice  of the Peace Russ Casey is white I  actually don't think that's the most  relevant sort of judicial system  disparity here the more relevant one is  that crystal Mason and Rosa Maria Ortega  are random people whereas Russ case is a  justice of the peace right he's part of  the system and I read an argument online  saying we'll hold on a second crystal  mason was already on probation when she  voted illegally that means her sentence  should be harsher  we're talking about a justice of the  peace who rigged their own election  that's a proactive action isn't that an  aggravating factor compared to  accidentally voting illegally there's  another argument I read which is well  Mason was a tax preparer years ago and  she inflated some returns for clients  that's a white-collar crime and I'm not  defending white-collar crime in fact  I've argued that white-collar crime is  not prosecuted harshly enough but you've  got to be consistent and crystal Mason  getting five years in prison when a  justice of the peace gets probation also  doesn't make sense and then we get to  the broader problem prosecutor Matt  Smith was asked about the sentencing  disparities and he said well the two  women Rosa Maria Ortega and crystal  Mason they didn't accept plea agreements  and they went to trial  Casey accepted a plea agreement that is  really really disturbing to me because  what the prosecutor is basically saying  is that the women ended up with harsher  sentences because they invoke their  constitutionally protected right to a  trial right and if the job of the  prosecutor is justice punishing people  for invoking their right to trial is  problematic at its base and we know that  that's how it works right we've talked  about the entire plea agreement  industrial complex it's a mechanism used  to put nonviolent drug offenders in  prison in huge numbers and that's  something we should all be concerned  about and then lastly there's the other  debate we've talked about this before  probably a bigger conversation than we  can have now which is should you be able  to vote when you're on probation  and sure to be able to vote when you're  in prison it's a broader debate what was  I remember you took a strong position on  that Pat although I don't remember what  it was oh I don't know I mean I'd have  to think about it a little bit you feel  like maybe once you're when you're in  prison I could see them taking away your  right to vote  you're okay certainly when you're on  probation or parole you should be able  to that's my take the counter argument  would be probation and parole are  privileges you could be in prison the  entire time so that your actions are  evaluated as if you were still in prison  like that's a moment they would make  once you're on probation or parole you  start to get some of your rights back  so I think voting can be one of them in  any case there's like three or four  different layers to this story and the  bottom line is five years probation for  a guy who rigged his own election yeah  and these are really harsh sentences  sentences to like five years in prison  or eight years in prison  yeah and it's no wonder why voter fraud  isn't really that prevalent because  those are really harsh sentences and  it's an exchange for one seemingly  inconsequential vote you're not gonna  change an election with that and so it  argues that three million people voted  illegally it's not very true what we've  seen is that number one very few people  actually do so-called in-person voter  fraud or illegal voting and that when  they do they're often trying to test the  system to prove how easy it is to vote  illegally and they end up getting caught  and being sentenced to prison like it's  the actual exact opposite narrative as  to what you would need to believe to  believe that three to five million  people voted illegally for Hillary  Clinton  we will follow whether there are appeals  here for sure the video you just watched  was made possible by support from you  please go to patreon.com slash david  pakman show you can make a pledge of as  little as one dollar per month and every  dollar helps to support the work that  we're doing particularly in light o